<BaseLayout title="How Subsidies Work at Lichen" description="Our Community & Care Subsidy Policy — how donors fill the well, and how Lichen identifies who is most in need of a drink.">
  <section class="container container--narrow pol">
    <a class="pol-back" href="/economy">&larr; Back to The Economy</a>
    <span class="eyebrow">Lichen</span>
    <h1 class="display h-1">How Subsidies Work at Lichen</h1>
    <p class="pol-sub">Our Community &amp; Care Subsidy Policy</p>
    <p class="pol-meta">Version 1 — adopted August 2026 · under ongoing review with our accountant</p>

    <p>Some members can afford everything they need on the platform. Many can't — or wouldn't think to try the thing that might change everything. Subsidies are how the community closes that gap: donors fill the well, and Lichen identifies who is most in need of a drink. This page explains exactly how that works, in plain language, because how money moves through a healing community should never be a mystery.</p>

    <h2 class="display h-2" id="members">If you're a member who needs support</h2>
    <p>When you join the platform and identify the need for a subsidy — whether it's care through Concierge, or a good, service, membership, event, or course in the community — you complete a <strong>Web of Wellbeing (WOW) assessment</strong>: a picture of your mental, physical, spiritual, social, environmental, and economic health. That's it. No means-testing interrogation, no proving you're poor enough or sick enough. Just an honest picture, proportionate to the size of the support.</p>
    <p>Your WOW stays inside Lichen. It is never sold and never shared outside the network. It exists for one purpose: to map the gap between what our current economy has given you and what a genuine exchange of value would — so support flows to where the imbalance is real.</p>
    <p>There are two doors in:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>"I can't afford it."</strong> The thing that would help exists, and money is the only barrier.</li>
      <li><strong>"I never would have tried it."</strong> You didn't know it existed, didn't believe it was for you, or come from a community that's never had access. A Lichen clinician or care coordinator sees a genuine fit — a first responder who's never tried somatic work, a caregiver who's never met a horse as a healer — and the community funds your first experience. This door matters to us as much as the first one: exposure is a resource too.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Every request gets a real look from a human steward, using written criteria, and every decision — yes or no — is recorded with its reasons. Not every request is approved; that's what makes an approval mean something. Subsidized care is identical to paid care, your practitioners aren't told who's subsidized unless it's operationally necessary, and nothing about your subsidy is ever visible to anyone who doesn't need to know — including the donors who funded it. What donors do receive is the aggregate story of their impact — this many organic garden beds planted, this much care subsidized, this many first experiences funded — real numbers, never names.</p>
    <p>There's one exception, and it belongs entirely to you: if how the subsidies you've received have positively impacted your life becomes a story you <em>want</em> the people who made it possible to hear, you can choose to share it — anonymously or by name, in your own words, on your own timeline. These full-circle moments are some of the most powerful medicine in the community, for teller and giver alike. But sharing is always an offering, never a repayment. No one will ever ask, expect, or hint — the gift was already complete.</p>

    <h2 class="display h-2" id="donors">If you're a donor</h2>
    <p>Here's the honest deal, and it's simpler than most nonprofits make it:</p>
    <p><strong>You choose the well.</strong> Give to Lichen generally, or designate your gift — to a program, a practitioner's fund ("subsidize Melanie's services"), or a purpose ("get this kind of good to this population"). If a designation doesn't fit our mission, we'll say so before accepting — never after. When we accept a designated gift, we honor it: your dollars go where you pointed them, always. If a designation ever becomes impossible or fully funded, we apply the remainder to the closest related purpose — and we tell you that up front, in writing, with every gift.</p>
    <p><strong>You never choose who drinks.</strong> No donor — however generous, however beloved — selects, approves, vetoes, or is told who receives subsidized support. Today, human stewards make every one of those calls against our written criteria; over time, they are teaching the mycelium's algorithm to see imbalance across the whole network, and they'll oversee it until it has earned the trust and intelligence to help automate what humans began. Either way, the principle never changes: the gift and the decision never sit in the same hands.</p>
    <p><strong>And a few bright lines that protect <em>you</em> as much as us:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>If you want to pay for a <em>specific person's</em> care or goods, we love that impulse — and it's a personal gift, not a charitable donation. No tax receipt, no deduction, and we'll help you structure it cleanly (often the simplest path is paying the practitioner directly).</li>
      <li>Gifts from donor-advised funds, private foundations, and IRA distributions can never carry personal benefits back to you — IRS rules, and we guard your tax position as carefully as our own.</li>
      <li>Your family and household members aren't eligible for subsidies traceable to your gifts. If a family member happens to qualify for a subsidy that you helped fund, they can still receive support — Lichen simply draws it from a different pool, general funds or another donor's gift, so your dollars never pay for your own family's care. This protects you from tax exposure you'd never see coming: when your tax-deducted dollars cover a relative's care, the IRS sees you buying something for your own household with pre-tax money — a personal benefit that can unwind your deduction, and with donor-advised fund gifts, trigger real penalties.</li>
      <li>A fund named for a practitioner buys care <em>through</em> them for members, at their standard rates — it's never a gift <em>to</em> them, and Lichen selects who receives that care like any other subsidy.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="display h-2">The boring-but-important part</h2>
    <p>Subsidies fund services that can be lawfully offered — by licensed professionals practicing within their scope, and by unlicensed healers offering what the law allows anyone to offer. Much of the world's best medicine has never needed a license, and it belongs here; where a modality is regulated, we work within the rules. Decision records are kept long enough to stand behind every decision we've made — our accountant sets the exact retention schedule — and a sample is reviewed with her annually.</p>

    <h2 class="display h-2">The principle underneath</h2>
    <p>The shadow of generosity is control — and this policy is how we keep that shadow named. Donors make healing possible; Lichen identifies where it's needed; members receive it freely, owing nothing to anyone but their own becoming.</p>
  </section>
